Q: (GNU) C23: How to define an constant array as a constant expression in C but keeping the defintion in a seperate C file? A: <p><code>extern const</code> doesn't make the value a compile-time constant in C, it just tells the compiler that the object is defined somewhere else, so <code>pin_definitions[12]</code> still can't be used where a constant expression is required.</p>
<p>If you only need the number of entries or a few values at compile time, I'd put those values in the header instead of trying to get them from the array:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// pin_definitions.h
#define PIN_COUNT 127

extern const pin_definition_t pin_definitions[PIN_COUNT];
</code></pre>
<p>Then</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// pin_definitions.c
const pin_definition_t pin_definitions[PIN_COUNT] = {
    ...
};
</code></pre>
<p>If you actually need to share the data between the <code>.c</code> files without having multiple copies of the table, an X-macro is one option, let's take a look</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// pin_data.def
X(0, 0x0000000a, 0xea)
X(1, 0x0000000f, 0x12)
</code></pre>
<p>You can include that file when defining the actual array:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#define X(id, reg, mode) { reg, mode },

const pin_definition_t pin_definitions[] = {
    #include &quot;pin_data.def&quot;
};

#undef X
</code></pre>
<p>And you can include the same data file elsewhere with a different definition of <code>X</code> if you need the values in another form.</p>
<p>If all you're trying to avoid is having a <code>static const</code> table duplicated in multiple translation units, I'd also check whether LTO is enough for your case, GCC can often remove/deduplicate identical read-only data when compiling with <code>-flto</code>, so you may not need the X-macro approach at all.</p>
